o with this general methodology in mind  i suggest that we now begin  at the end with the final link in this  chain of events  the mouse click and what better way for  our machine to click a mouse  than to mimic the motion of a human  index finger  from our list of design elements it  seems that the bell crank is best suited  to this purpose  as it will allow us to convert a  horizontal pull  into a vertical push using the relative  lengths of the bell crank's two arms to  control both the applied force  and the range of motion but a proper  mouse click involves both a downward  push and a release  how can we get the bell crank to return  to its original position after each push  huh this looks like a job for a torsion  spring  let's try the common type of torsion  spring configuration that's used in  clothes pins and mouse traps  a simple wire coil with a perpendicular  leg on each end  okay we've now used two design elements  the bell crank and torsion spring  and combined them into a sub-assembly  which we'll call the mouse  clicker and here's my computer model  which translates this sketch into a  buildable device  and can be used to examine its functions  more closely  the output of the mouse clicker is  obvious but what input will it require  well to operate properly the lower end  of the bell crank must receive a  moderate horizontal pulling force  applied through a distance of perhaps a  quarter to a half an inch  but only for a short period of time then  this force must decrease to near  zero so the torsion spring can return  the bell crank to its original position  thus releasing the mouse button okay  that's  two down and 18 more design elements to  go  after getting rid of my first sketch we  can now focus on the hydraulic system  which seems like a particularly  challenging element to incorporate into  this design  our tried and true approach is to use a  pair of water-filled  plastic syringes which will actually  serve as the actuator and hydraulic ram  as they did in our lecture about  hydraulic machinery this system will  provide an effective means of  transmitting force from the actuator to  the ram through a plastic tube  of any length we choose our challenge is  that the  input to this system must be a  relatively large steady force  applied to the actuator how might we  generate such a force within our machine  well suppose we add a lever like this  with a heavy  counterweight fixed to its short end the  lever is configured such that  after it rotates through 90 degrees the  counterweight will come to rest  on the plunger of the first syringe thus  applying the steady force required to  actuate the hydraulic system  the lever's long arm is provided so that  a trigger placed at its tip can hold the  arm in its initial position with minimal  force  thus the force required to release this  trigger will also be small  well by now you've probably noticed that  this combination of lever  and counterweight bears a very strong  resemblance to our trebuchet  this similarity suggests that a modified  version of our trebuchet  trigger will work here too we'll  configure this trigger  such that it can be released by a small  downward force  perhaps a falling object striking its  horizontal arm  we've now used three more design  elements the lever  counterweight and hydraulic system and  combine them into another sub assembly  which can be activated by a small  downward force on the trebuchet  trigger and which produces as output the  steady force  and linear motion of the hydraulic ram  next let's turn  to another challenging design element  hydro power  now clearly we'll want to use an  elevated water reservoir  serving as a source of potential energy  to power the water jet  as we did in our water turbine project  and because the reservoir must be  elevated  we can support it with a braced frame  tower and thus  check off another design element but how  can we switch on the water jet  well in the interest of simplicity we  can use a simple stopper to control the  flow from the outlet tube as we did in  our turbine project  but how will we generate the substantial  force required to pull the stopper out  of the tube  recognizing that a stretched spring can  develop a substantial internal force  and a lever can magnify a force let's  try combining a linear spring  and a lever to meet this need now  initially we'll hold the system in  position with a thin  wire attached to a fixed support  when the wire is cut the stretched  spring will pull the stopper from the  outlet tube  and it will also pull the lever through  a full 90 degrees of rotation  thus moving the stopper clear of the  water jet so it won't interfere with the  jet's operation  okay this is another nicely packaged  sub-assembly  which incorporates water power a braced  frame  a linear spring and another lever and  here's the corresponding computer model  which illustrates the required input  cutting the wire that holds the  spring-loaded lever in position  and the output a force applied by the  water jet  most likely to a vertical surface  positioned immediately in front of the  jet  next how shall we incorporate a design  element  that demonstrates aerodynamic stability  and we know a lot about achieving  stability in the design of a rocket  though obviously we won't be able to  ignite a rocket engine here in the  studio  wait i have an idea suppose that we hung  a small model rocket from a string  to create a pendulum we could arrange  this sub-assembly such that  if the rocket is stable it will hit a  target of some sort at the extreme  opposite end of the pendulum swing but  if it's unstable it will turn  sideways and miss the target  to make this concept work we'll need a  release mechanism for the rocket  let's try a simple retainer pin that  passes through a wooden block  and engages with a wire eye fixed to the  back of the rocket  ok we can now check off the pendulum and  aerodynamic stability  as input the 