he closing of the  electrical circuit to start the motor  now with the propeller spinning the boom  will rotate through 45 degrees  from its initial position to the point  at which the sail moves beyond the  airstream  and stops developing lift the larger of  our two gears will rotate along with the  boom  and because we want the smaller gear to  rotate through 180 degrees  as the larger one rotates through 45  degrees  we'll use a gear ratio of one to four  the cord that activates the mouse  clicker is mounted one half inch from  the center of the smaller gear  as shown here thus as this gear rotates  through 180 degrees  it'll first pull the cord a half inch to  click the mouse  and then after the 180 degree rotation  is complete  there will be no more force on the sail  and therefore no tension in the cord  so the mouse click will be released and  at its peak  the magnitude of this pulling force will  be roughly 10 times  larger than the aerodynamic force  applied to the sail  well this is a very important  sub-assembly  not only because it will use seven of  our design elements  the motor electric circuit propeller  sail aerodynamic lift gear train and  pulley  but also because it has addressed the  critical issue of how to activate the  mouse clicker  okay at this point there are only three  unused design elements remaining in our  list  we'll certainly be able to use the beam  and suspension bridge to provide  structural support to some of the other  design elements  but the lighter than air object presents  another significant  challenge we can easily include this  element in our machine by using a  helium-filled balloon  but what will it actually do the net  buoyant force developed by a standard  size helium balloon is only about 1 10  of an ounce  we can't possibly use such a small force  to pull a trigger  or throw a switch ah  but we could use it to complete our  electrical circuit  in a more creative way let's call this  a balloon switch we'll start  wit